  "style": {
    "id": "reaction-diffusion",
    "name": "Reaction-Diffusion",
    "description": "Rules, not a plan: a macro shot of a shallow glass petri dish on a pale lab bench, filled with cream gel in which a deep-teal pattern grows. Five drops land; each spreads into a ring that pinches into spots, the spots split again and again, fronts from different drops meet and lock into one winding maze. The pattern is a real Gray–Scott reaction-diffusion simulation (two substances, 9-point Laplacian, explicit Euler, seeded drops) run once on the CPU at page load; the activator field is stored every 0.25 s of video and the gel shader blends the two neighbouring states, B-spline-filters them, raises the surface from the same value (wet glossy relief) and optionally tints each drop's territory. Soft drop plinks, then a quiet granular crackle whose density follows how much of the dish is patterned and still changing.",
